First of all,  the main fear I have of cockatrices is not *fighting*
them but in handling/stumbling over it's corpse after it's dead. In
this you and I are in 100% agreement.

As towards fighting them ...

Cockatrices are slow, poorly armored, and have few hit points.   This
makes them ideal fodder for arrows, crossbow bolts, darts, daggers,
applied polearms, or even thrown rocks.   Given this fact, why should
one close to melee with one unless one has no choice?   Especially if
one has missile/polearm skills in need of advancing?

While I agree that the chances of any *individual* cockatrice stone
attack succeeding are insignificant, the *cumulative* probability over
the course of the game of suffering at least one stone attack
approaches 1.0 the more hits you allow them to make.

Even a stone attack that fails is still problematic.

1)  First of all, loss of speed intrinsic ("you are slowing down") is
your first warning of a successful stone attack.  This is not a good
thing to have happen, especially if it happens in melee combat with
other creatures at the same time.  Yes, it can be fixed.  But why give
yourself an additional problem you don't need?

2) If a stone attack succeeds, I bet I know what you're going to be
doing in at least the next turn.  You're going to be eating a lizard
corpse when you *could* be doing something more productive.  Ceding
initiative to an opponent in melee combat is never a good idea.

Yes, I agree a cockatrice poses little threat in melee to a prepared
character.  However, it poses almost zero threat when engaged with
missile weapons.  Why pass up the opportunity to train your favorite
missile weapon against an ideal practice target in favor of  engaging
in melee combat against an opponent whose special attack can be
problematic, at the least? 

